    "prompt": "Below is a description of the Python function `GenerateSchema.match_type`.\n\nDescription:\n\"\"\"\nThe `match_type` function in the `pydantic._internal._generate_schema` module is responsible for generating a schema for a given Python type. This schema is used by Pydantic to validate and serialize data. The function takes a single parameter, `obj`, which is the type for which the schema is being generated. The function returns a `core_schema.CoreSchema` object that represents the schema for the given type.\n\nThe function handles a wide range of types, including built-in types like `str`, `int`, and `float`, as well as more complex types like `datetime.date`, `Decimal`, and `UUID`. It also handles generic types, type aliases, and dataclasses. For each type, the function calls a corresponding helper method to generate the schema. For example, it calls `_str_schema` for `str` types, `_int_schema` for `int` types, and so on.\n\nThe function also handles edge cases, such as when the type is `None` or when the type is not recognized. In these cases, it returns a schema that allows any type or raises an error, respectively. The function does not raise exceptions for unrecognized types; instead, it returns a schema that allows any type if the `_arbitrary_types` flag is set, or a schema that represents an unknown type otherwise.\n\nThe function relies on several helper methods and functions, such as `_fraction_schema`, `_tuple_schema`, `_list_schema`, `_dict_schema`, `_type_alias_type_schema`, `_type_schema`, `_literal_schema`, `_typed_dict_schema`, `_namedtuple_schema`, `_pattern_schema`, `_hashable_schema`, `_unsubstituted_typevar_schema`, `_call_schema`, `_enum_schema`, and `_zoneinfo_schema`. These helper methods are responsible for generating the schema for specific types.\n\nThe function does not have any side effects. It does not modify any global state or external resources. It simply generates and returns a schema for the given type.\n\"\"\"\n\nSplit the description into atomic claims. Each claim must be a single, self-contained factual statement about the function that could be checked against the function's source code (name the function explicitly; no pronouns referring to other claims). Do not add claims that are not in the description; do not merge two facts into one claim. Keep the original wording where possible. Assign each claim exactly one type:\n- \"structural\": the function's signature, parameter names/defaults/types, return type, or which functions, methods, classes or attributes it uses.\n- \"dataflow\": how a value is computed, transformed, passed on, or what is returned for which input.\n- \"behavioural\": exceptions raised or caught, conditions/early returns, side effects, edge-case handling, ordering/iteration behaviour.\n- \"intent\": purpose, rationale, or the role of the function in the larger system.\n\nReturn a JSON array of at most 12 objects: [{\"text\": \"...\", \"type\": \"structural|dataflow|behavioural|intent\"}, ...]",
